1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:04,000
Egypt's pyramids.

2
00:00:04,001 --> 00:00:10,000
The greatest of these monuments was the tallest structure on Earth for over 4,000 years.

3
00:00:10,001 --> 00:00:16,000
They contained gigantic stone blocks, weighing up to 70 tons.

4
00:00:16,001 --> 00:00:20,000
Yet they were made by a society that didn't even possess the wheel.

5
00:00:20,001 --> 00:00:24,000
Now, Egyptian Pyramids on Modern Marvels.

6
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:43,720
Inside the famous Great Pyramid of Khufu at Giza, a series of mysterious passageways lead through the Mountain of Stone.

7
00:00:44,460 --> 00:00:51,980
Passing through a magnificent grand gallery, the main passageway finally ends at a granite-lined vault, deep within the massive structure.

8
00:00:52,780 --> 00:00:58,340
Called the King's Chamber, many scholars believe that this is where the Great Khufu was finally laid to rest.

9
00:00:58,820 --> 00:00:59,520
But was he?

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,160
For centuries, these extraordinary edifices have inspired an enduring fascination in people

11
00:01:07,161 --> 00:01:11,900
around the world. Generations have wanted to see them, study them, and unlock their

12
00:01:11,901 --> 00:01:16,014
age-old secrets, such as why and exactly how they were built.

13
00:01:16,015 --> 00:01:17,700
And yet, despite the best

14
00:01:17,701 --> 00:01:22,480
efforts of researchers, the solutions to many questions surrounding the pyramids remain

15
00:01:22,481 --> 00:01:23,768
tantalizingly obscure.

16
00:01:23,769 --> 00:01:28,040
The lack of concrete evidence from ancient Egyptian sites has complicated

17
00:01:28,041 --> 00:01:28,828
the problem.

18
00:01:28,829 --> 00:01:33,820
Even the application of the most modern technology has failed to unravel all

19
00:01:33,821 --> 00:01:34,631
the mysteries.

20
00:01:34,632 --> 00:01:38,860
Recently, however, new theories have been proposed which call some of the

21
00:01:38,861 --> 00:01:44,600
key generally accepted ideas about the pyramids into question, and that may indeed solve some

22
00:01:44,601 --> 00:01:47,956
of the riddles concerning these magnificent achievements.

23
00:01:47,957 --> 00:01:49,840
One of the first mysteries about

24
00:01:49,841 --> 00:01:54,785
the pyramids is their exact number. Nearly 100 have been discovered in Egypt.

25
00:01:54,786 --> 00:01:55,620
Surprisingly,

26
00:01:55,621 --> 00:01:59,620
Interestingly, however, at least three pyramids that are mentioned in inscriptions remain

27
00:01:59,621 --> 00:02:02,620
obscured by time and the relentless desert sands.

28
00:02:02,621 --> 00:02:07,620
It may seem surprising that pyramids have not been located yet, but over thousands of

29
00:02:07,621 --> 00:02:12,620
years, their superstructures may have been quarried away and all that's left is underground

30
00:02:12,621 --> 00:02:14,620
or may have been built over.

31
00:02:14,621 --> 00:02:22,620
Built during a span of almost a thousand years, from approximately 2630 to 1640 BC, the pyramids

32
00:02:22,621 --> 00:02:25,620
The pyramids stand as cultural and engineering marvels

33
00:02:25,621 --> 00:02:27,620
of staggering proportions.

34
00:02:27,621 --> 00:02:30,620
Not only are they incredibly magnificent structures,

35
00:02:30,621 --> 00:02:34,620
but of course there are also the few wonders of the ancient world to survive.

36
00:02:34,621 --> 00:02:39,620
The purpose of the pyramid is to serve as the king's tomb,

37
00:02:39,621 --> 00:02:42,620
but that's only the beginning because we have to understand

38
00:02:42,621 --> 00:02:46,620
why the king of Egypt needed to have a tomb quite like that.

39
00:02:46,621 --> 00:02:51,620
The pyramid is the architectural realization of the king's ascent to the sun.

40
00:02:51,621 --> 00:02:54,620
That was the king's after death fate.

41
00:02:54,621 --> 00:02:57,620
The pyramid is the spot in which that great transformation takes place

42
00:02:57,621 --> 00:03:00,620
in which the king is reborn.

43
00:03:02,620 --> 00:03:04,620
An ancient Arab proverb reads,

44
00:03:04,621 --> 00:03:10,620
Man fears time, but time fears the pyramids.

45
00:03:10,621 --> 00:03:13,620
And yet the so-called golden age of pyramid building

46
00:03:13,621 --> 00:03:16,620
lasted for only 150 years,

47
00:03:16,621 --> 00:03:19,620
during the fourth dynasty of the Egyptian Old Kingdom.

48
00:03:19,621 --> 00:03:24,620
And the greatest of the pyramids, those on the Giza Plateau near Cairo, were constructed

49
00:03:24,621 --> 00:03:27,620
over a span of only three generations.

50
00:03:27,621 --> 00:03:32,620
Remarkable engineering achievements in their own right, the pyramids were also the centerpieces

51
00:03:32,621 --> 00:03:37,620
of often vast complexes of temples and smaller tombs, called necropolis.

52
00:03:37,621 --> 00:03:40,620
The word necropolis literally means city of the dead.

53
00:03:40,621 --> 00:03:46,620
So with the pyramid itself, we only have the monument over the king's tomb.

54
00:03:46,621 --> 00:03:48,620
Attached to the pyramid is a temple.

55
00:03:48,621 --> 00:03:50,620
Then there's a long causeway that leads to the valley

56
00:03:50,621 --> 00:03:52,620
where there's another temple.

57
00:03:52,621 --> 00:03:56,620
Offerings are presented at the statues of the king in this temple.

58
00:03:56,621 --> 00:04:00,620
There is enormous staff that's involved for the upkeep of the temple.

59
00:04:00,621 --> 00:04:04,620
The legendary Sphinx, also at Giza,

60
00:04:04,621 --> 00:04:06,620
is part of one of these necropolies.

61
00:04:06,621 --> 00:04:10,620
That built around the pyramid of King Khufu's son, Khafre.

62
00:04:10,621 --> 00:04:14,620
We may know the reason why the pyramids were built,

63
00:04:14,621 --> 00:04:17,820
but the origin of their specific shape is still uncertain.

64
00:04:19,620 --> 00:04:23,540
The earliest Egyptian burials involved placing the body in a shallow pit,

65
00:04:24,000 --> 00:04:26,740
then covering it with a large rock or mound of stones.

66
00:04:27,260 --> 00:04:30,060
These not only served to keep jackals from digging up the dead,

67
00:04:30,640 --> 00:04:33,640
but also were probably meant to represent the primordial mound

68
00:04:33,641 --> 00:04:36,260
from which the Egyptians believed all life sprang.

69
00:04:37,280 --> 00:04:41,620
The ancient Egyptians were oriented towards the Nile,

70
00:04:41,621 --> 00:04:44,820
which was the source of their life and their sustenance.

71
00:04:45,460 --> 00:04:47,740
And the Nile used to flood every year.

72
00:04:48,180 --> 00:04:51,000
After that was over, when the floods would recede,

73
00:04:51,240 --> 00:04:53,120
the land would become visible again.

74
00:04:53,340 --> 00:04:56,040
So that became a metaphor for creation.

75
00:04:56,720 --> 00:05:01,860
So the primeval mound was the first time the first land began to be seen

76
00:05:01,861 --> 00:05:05,480
when the first waters, the primeval waters, had begun to recede.

77
00:05:05,780 --> 00:05:09,780
And it was upon this primeval mound that the sun rose for the first time.

78
00:05:09,781 --> 00:05:12,881
Then this mound actually is a pyramid.

79
00:05:12,882 --> 00:05:19,980
It represents the primordial mound that the God created whole Egypt through this mound.

80
00:05:20,820 --> 00:05:29,840
The symbol of this primordial mound was the sacred benben, a pyramidal-shaped stone that most Egyptologists believe would later influence the form of the pyramids.

81
00:05:29,841 --> 00:05:34,280
And in the top of the pyramid, the capstone is a bimbin.

82
00:05:34,960 --> 00:05:37,820
Then the capstone that they put it in the top of the pyramid,

83
00:05:38,240 --> 00:05:42,680
and they cased it with electrum, white gold and yellow gold.

84
00:05:43,140 --> 00:05:47,120
And that capstone is a symbol that the pyramid is finished.

85
00:05:48,060 --> 00:05:51,960
But some other scholars cite additional influences for the shape of the pyramids.

86
00:05:52,460 --> 00:05:55,040
One Egyptologist has a provocative new theory,

87
00:05:55,041 --> 00:05:59,820
based on a migration of desert people into the Nile Valley over 5,000 years ago.

88
00:05:59,840 --> 00:06:02,640
at exactly the time when pyramids began to be built.

89
00:06:03,800 --> 00:06:07,840
According to the theory, the newcomers brought knowledge of the desert landscape with them

90
00:06:07,841 --> 00:06:09,740
that might have affected the pyramids' form.

91
00:06:10,660 --> 00:06:17,860
A very important thing in the Western Desert is the fact that the only landforms that persist everywhere

92
00:06:17,861 --> 00:06:21,560
are conical or pyramidal landforms.

93
00:06:22,300 --> 00:06:28,240
Pointed hills is the main persisting landscape.

94
00:06:28,241 --> 00:06:36,560
These are three conical structures in the middle of the desert, some 300 miles west of the Nile.

95
00:06:37,200 --> 00:06:46,520
The ancient Egyptians would have seen these things and would have wanted to build their structures that would remain in the windy environment in that form.

96
00:06:47,200 --> 00:06:52,940
But these natural landforms can definitely become, in my mind, the precursors to the pyramids.

97
00:06:58,240 --> 00:07:05,160
But before actual pyramids were built, Egyptian tombs went through another stage of development, the mastaba.

98
00:07:06,520 --> 00:07:10,700
Mastabas were low trapezoidal structures with sloping sides and flat roofs.

99
00:07:11,480 --> 00:07:15,260
Usually made of mud bricks, they developed from the primordial mound burials.

100
00:07:16,160 --> 00:07:20,260
Mastaba, and it's the Arabic word for bench, but they're bigger than any bench.

101
00:07:20,560 --> 00:07:24,700
But the mastaba is the word for the superstructure of a private tomb.

102
00:07:25,120 --> 00:07:27,780
The Egyptian tomb really consists of two parts.

103
00:07:27,781 --> 00:07:31,080
There's the below ground part, which is the actual burial chamber,

104
00:07:31,320 --> 00:07:34,320
and there is the above ground part, which could be cut into the cliff,

105
00:07:34,480 --> 00:07:37,760
or it could be a constructed monument like a mastaba.

106
00:07:38,460 --> 00:07:42,700
With mastabas, the Egyptians had taken the first step towards the classic pyramid.

107
00:07:43,220 --> 00:07:47,560
The next stage came around 2630 BC, during the Egyptian Third Dynasty,

108
00:07:48,040 --> 00:07:49,380
during the reign of King Djoser,

109
00:07:49,820 --> 00:07:52,740
when Djoser's high priest and architect, Imhotep,

110
00:07:53,100 --> 00:07:55,760
began building the famous step pyramid at Saqqara.

111
00:07:55,761 --> 00:08:01,204
In Dynasty 3, which is before Dynasty 4, there was a genius architect.

112
00:08:01,205 --> 00:08:02,760
His name is Imhotem.

113
00:08:02,761 --> 00:08:10,760
And he began to make a revolution in building, changing the building from mud brick to limestone.

114
00:08:10,761 --> 00:08:18,760
And he built what's called the Step Pyramid, a mastapa above the one, until he reached six mastapas.

115
00:08:18,761 --> 00:08:21,760
And that was a revolution.

116
00:08:21,761 --> 00:08:30,480
Soaring to almost 200 feet in height, with a base measuring 397 by 358 feet, the Step

117
00:08:30,481 --> 00:08:34,980
Pyramid is thought by many scholars to be the first large building in human history made

118
00:08:34,981 --> 00:08:40,120
completely of stone, and the first pyramid of any kind built in Egypt.

119
00:08:40,121 --> 00:08:45,240
Imhotep remains one of the few overseers of a pyramid whose name is still known.

120
00:08:45,241 --> 00:08:48,520
Exactly how he designed and planned the structure is not.

121
00:08:48,521 --> 00:08:53,960
We have from later times papyrus where they planned a temple.

122
00:08:54,540 --> 00:08:56,940
And they knew in advance how many people they would need,

123
00:08:57,440 --> 00:09:00,460
how many food they would have to bring to the place,

124
00:09:00,660 --> 00:09:02,120
and how many stones would be needed.

125
00:09:02,680 --> 00:09:06,300
And so there must have been similar papers for all these pyramids,

126
00:09:06,700 --> 00:09:09,020
but unfortunately we don't have them.

127
00:09:10,500 --> 00:09:13,880
However, we do know how the workmen actually accomplished the construction.

128
00:09:13,881 --> 00:09:23,880
To move the stone they used levers, wedges and rope, usually pulled by teams of men,

129
00:09:23,881 --> 00:09:29,780
chanting, heaving at a one, two, three, heave sort of thing.

130
00:09:29,781 --> 00:09:36,560
To cut the stone they used copper chisels, which are softer than modern iron chisels,

131
00:09:36,561 --> 00:09:39,380
but limestone is a soft stone.

132
00:09:39,381 --> 00:09:43,040
Of course, it takes a lot of work, but they had the manpower to do it.

133
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:49,740
The result is a monument that is as precise as it is imposing.

134
00:09:50,480 --> 00:09:53,180
Despite the remarkable symmetry of the final structure, however,

135
00:09:53,780 --> 00:09:57,980
archaeological investigations have proven that Imhotep did not plan for the monument

136
00:09:57,981 --> 00:09:59,560
to be a step pyramid from the beginning.

137
00:09:59,880 --> 00:10:03,400
He started with the mastaba, then he built a larger mastaba,

138
00:10:03,700 --> 00:10:06,160
and then he started piling them one on top of the other,

139
00:10:06,240 --> 00:10:08,860
so you have as though you have a stairway to heaven.

140
00:10:09,380 --> 00:10:14,380
Why the Steppe Pyramid was enlarged in these stages remains unknown.

141
00:10:14,381 --> 00:10:19,380
As magnificent an edifice as the Steppe Pyramid was, few later pharaohs copied it.

142
00:10:19,381 --> 00:10:25,380
Instead, they soon moved on to a new form, one that provided new challenges to their builders,

143
00:10:25,381 --> 00:10:30,380
and that would change the shape of the pyramids forever.

144
00:10:32,380 --> 00:10:37,380
Just over a half century after King Djoser built the Steppe Pyramid in 2630 BC,

145
00:10:37,381 --> 00:10:40,120
King Sneferu came to the throne of Egypt.

146
00:10:40,121 --> 00:10:43,060
He would become the greatest pyramid builder

147
00:10:43,061 --> 00:10:44,398
in Egyptian history.

148
00:10:44,399 --> 00:10:48,680
Unsatisfied with his early efforts, Sneferu erected no less than

149
00:10:48,681 --> 00:10:53,768
three huge pyramids during a reign that may have lasted up to 50 years.

150
00:10:53,769 --> 00:10:54,700
The first was

151
00:10:54,701 --> 00:11:00,466
the so-called Collapsed Pyramid at Meidoum, begun around 2575 BC.

152
00:11:00,467 --> 00:11:02,240
During its building,

153
00:11:02,241 --> 00:11:06,433
Sneferu decided to change the form of the Collapsed Pyramid several times.

154
00:11:06,434 --> 00:11:07,134
Why he did

155
00:11:07,135 --> 00:11:11,560
so remains unknown. How he did it is understood much more completely.

156
00:11:11,561 --> 00:11:18,159
It was built as a step pyramid originally, which was then increased in size.

157
00:11:18,160 --> 00:11:18,940
Then King

158
00:11:18,941 --> 00:11:25,540
Sneferu, the inventor of the true pyramid, decided to change it into four smooth sides

159
00:11:25,541 --> 00:11:30,385
that met at a point high in the sky.

160
00:11:30,386 --> 00:11:36,980
And for me, that was the most amazing feat that I

161
00:11:36,981 --> 00:11:38,683
seen in my studies.

162
00:11:38,684 --> 00:11:44,600
Because if you deviate the angle of one side, even a little bit on

163
00:11:44,601 --> 00:11:50,080
the bottom, it will be exaggerated as you rise to the top. And they never meet. You

164
00:11:50,081 --> 00:11:53,160
never get to the pyramidion, to the apex.

165
00:11:53,161 --> 00:11:59,580
Today, all that is visible of this pyramid is part of its inner stepped core, surrounded

166
00:11:59,581 --> 00:12:04,146
by heaps of loose rubble, thus giving it its collapsed appearance.

167
00:12:04,147 --> 00:12:06,360
In earlier times, archaeologists

168
00:12:06,361 --> 00:12:11,040
believed that it had indeed collapsed during construction and was for this reason abandoned.

169
00:12:11,740 --> 00:12:16,520
But later research indicates that the pyramid was successfully completed, then was systematically

170
00:12:16,521 --> 00:12:17,951
stripped of its casing.

171
00:12:17,952 --> 00:12:22,180
Over the ages, that exterior was quarried, used for other buildings.

172
00:12:22,620 --> 00:12:26,600
Whole cities could be built from the stone that had been gathered there for the pyramids,

173
00:12:27,140 --> 00:12:30,501
and that accounts for its present appearance.

174
00:12:30,502 --> 00:12:34,160
Like the step pyramid completed 55 years earlier,

175
00:12:34,161 --> 00:12:38,960
the core of the collapsed pyramid was made with layers of stone that actually leaned inward.

176
00:12:40,360 --> 00:12:45,240
Step pyramid of Maedum was built with what was called accretion layers.

177
00:12:46,100 --> 00:12:51,260
They are layers of stone that are built circumferentially around a core,

178
00:12:51,980 --> 00:12:54,200
and they lean in, actually.

179
00:12:56,760 --> 00:13:00,960
One thing that is known for certain is that at some point during its construction,

180
00:13:00,961 --> 00:13:03,840
the collapsed pyramid was temporarily abandoned.

181
00:13:03,841 --> 00:13:10,500
For reason that we do not know, in year 17, he left this pyramid completely unfinished.

182
00:13:11,180 --> 00:13:16,340
And he built in the shore the pyramid that we call it now the Bent Pyramid.

183
00:13:20,240 --> 00:13:23,360
The Bent Pyramid is really constructed.

184
00:13:24,160 --> 00:13:27,280
They started 54 degrees and they could not continue.

185
00:13:27,420 --> 00:13:28,700
They had to switch the angle.

186
00:13:28,701 --> 00:13:34,700
The Bent was the first pyramid designed from the beginning to be a true smooth-sided pyramid.

187
00:13:34,701 --> 00:13:39,700
Part of the reason it still looks that way is because, unlike almost all the other pyramids,

188
00:13:39,701 --> 00:13:42,700
most of its exterior casing stones remain in place.

189
00:13:42,701 --> 00:13:48,700
With a base measuring 617 feet square and a height of 345 feet,

190
00:13:48,701 --> 00:13:53,700
the Bent pyramid is considerably larger than both the collapsed pyramid and the step pyramid.

191
00:13:53,701 --> 00:13:57,700
However, soon after construction began, problems appeared.

192
00:13:57,701 --> 00:14:00,000
problems that are still visible in the structure today.

193
00:14:00,660 --> 00:14:06,140
They built up ways and discovered that cracks were beginning to form,

194
00:14:06,880 --> 00:14:08,320
internally and externally.

195
00:14:09,140 --> 00:14:13,840
So about halfway up, they changed the angle of the rise of the sides.

196
00:14:15,180 --> 00:14:18,840
The problem was the pyramid was built on a clay subsoil.

197
00:14:19,600 --> 00:14:21,660
It couldn't support the weight of the pyramid,

198
00:14:21,920 --> 00:14:24,960
and that was the reason for the cracking, but they didn't know it at the time.

199
00:14:24,961 --> 00:14:31,640
The method of laying the masonry on the upper portion of the pyramid was also modified.

200
00:14:31,641 --> 00:14:35,100
Instead of the earlier technique of the stones leaning inward to match the angle of the outer

201
00:14:35,101 --> 00:14:40,420
slope, the later courses were assembled in nearly horizontal layers.

202
00:14:40,421 --> 00:14:44,640
This was not only easier to accomplish for the workers and masons who built the pyramid,

203
00:14:44,641 --> 00:14:47,580
but also made the structure far more stable.

204
00:14:47,581 --> 00:14:51,600
With these engineering lessons learned, Snefru then undertook the building of a brand new

205
00:14:51,601 --> 00:14:55,620
pyramid, one that is still hailed as among the most beautiful and perfect

206
00:14:55,621 --> 00:14:58,280
pyramids ever constructed, the Red Pyramid.

207
00:14:58,281 --> 00:15:04,140
They went just one kilometer to the north and built this massive stone

208
00:15:04,141 --> 00:15:09,540
building behind us which is called the Red Pyramid because of its appearance,

209
00:15:09,541 --> 00:15:17,880
which is better to be seen in the morning light when all these stones really have a red touch.

210
00:15:21,600 --> 00:15:30,700
With a base 772 feet square and a height of 345 feet, the Red Pyramid is more massive,

211
00:15:30,701 --> 00:15:32,900
but no taller than the Bent Pyramid.

212
00:15:32,901 --> 00:15:36,980
With an angle of approximately 43 degrees, the slope of the Red Pyramid is also much

213
00:15:36,981 --> 00:15:39,780
shallower than the lower portion of its bent cousin.

214
00:15:39,781 --> 00:15:46,480
The angle was an attempt to copy the upper angle of the Bent Pyramid because they felt

215
00:15:46,481 --> 00:15:51,360
that that was the safest angle to build a pyramid with.

216
00:15:51,361 --> 00:15:55,760
But structurally, the Red Pyramid's most important feature was the way in which its stones were

217
00:15:55,761 --> 00:15:56,760
assembled.

218
00:15:56,761 --> 00:16:02,700
The Red Pyramid was an advance over the earlier pyramids because the entire pyramid was built

219
00:16:02,701 --> 00:16:07,540
with courses of horizontal stone, a much more stable structure.

220
00:16:07,541 --> 00:16:13,360
All the forces are directly down instead of angled outwardly.

221
00:16:13,361 --> 00:16:19,840
The Red Pyramid of Sneferu is the first pyramid that is actually a perfect pyramid, that has

222
00:16:19,841 --> 00:16:24,960
achieved the pure abstract pyramidal form.

223
00:16:24,961 --> 00:16:30,180
No one knows exactly why Sneferu built this edifice, but archaeologists have speculated

224
00:16:30,181 --> 00:16:34,720
that he wanted his final resting place to be an ideal expression of the pyramid, and

225
00:16:34,721 --> 00:16:38,200
the Red Pyramid is certainly that.

226
00:16:38,201 --> 00:16:42,520
With the construction of the Red Pyramid, all the major engineering and structural elements

227
00:16:42,521 --> 00:16:45,600
of the classic pyramid were put in place.

228
00:16:45,601 --> 00:16:51,040
The stage was set for Snefru's son, Khufu, to build the greatest pyramid of them all.

229
00:16:54,400 --> 00:17:00,440
Near the modern city of Cairo lies the largest and most imposing concentration of pyramids in the world,

230
00:17:01,060 --> 00:17:02,420
those of the Giza Plateau.

231
00:17:03,240 --> 00:17:09,420
They include the biggest of them all, the Great Pyramid of Khufu, begun around 2551 B.C.,

232
00:17:09,421 --> 00:17:11,580
the Pyramid of Khufu's son, Khafre,

233
00:17:12,160 --> 00:17:15,140
the Pyramid of Khufu's grandson, Menkare,

234
00:17:15,600 --> 00:17:22,600
several smaller pyramids called Queen's Pyramids, and the legendary Sphinx.

235
00:17:22,601 --> 00:17:27,600
The site of Giza was uniquely suited for the construction of pyramids.

236
00:17:27,601 --> 00:17:34,600
It has the best quality of limestone. It's about 300 feet above the sea level.

237
00:17:34,601 --> 00:17:38,600
It's a perfect place. It has the best quality of the stones.

238
00:17:38,601 --> 00:17:42,600
And this type of stones never existed any place else.

239
00:17:45,600 --> 00:17:52,380
Giza was also less than a mile from the banks of the Nile, making the movement of manpower

240
00:17:52,381 --> 00:17:54,600
and supplies to the site far easier.

241
00:17:54,601 --> 00:17:58,560
The Nile was the great artery of ancient Egypt.

242
00:17:58,561 --> 00:18:02,880
There were no roads per se, so the river was instrumental.

243
00:18:02,881 --> 00:18:07,580
Stone was shipped over the river in barges, not only limestone from one side of the river

244
00:18:07,581 --> 00:18:11,160
to the other side of the river, but granite which came from hundreds of miles away to

245
00:18:11,161 --> 00:18:14,040
the south.

246
00:18:14,041 --> 00:18:18,660
construction of the Great Pyramid could begin, its builders had to find a way to make the

247
00:18:18,661 --> 00:18:24,432
site completely flat. Many Egyptologists believe that water was used.

248
00:18:24,433 --> 00:18:26,440
First, a shallow channel

249
00:18:26,441 --> 00:18:29,766
was dug around the site, then it was filled with water.

250
00:18:29,767 --> 00:18:31,580
Since the surface of the water

251
00:18:31,581 --> 00:18:35,720
would be exactly the same height in every part of the channel, the Egyptians only had

252
00:18:35,721 --> 00:18:39,002
to flatten the dry portions down to the level of the water.

253
00:18:39,003 --> 00:18:40,560
But not everyone agrees that

254
00:18:40,561 --> 00:18:44,780
this is how the site for the Great Pyramid was leveled.

255
00:18:44,781 --> 00:18:48,960
Martin Eisler is a sculptor who has studied Egyptian construction techniques for over

256
00:18:48,961 --> 00:18:49,960
30 years.

257
00:18:49,961 --> 00:18:54,440
He has several provocative theories about the building of the pyramids, which are supported

258
00:18:54,441 --> 00:18:58,600
by decades of painstaking research and observation.

259
00:18:58,601 --> 00:19:05,620
I do not think that the Great Pyramid was leveled by means of water in any form.

260
00:19:05,621 --> 00:19:12,280
I think they did it with no more complicated a device than this instrument called a square

261
00:19:12,281 --> 00:19:14,120
level.

262
00:19:14,121 --> 00:19:18,760
It's actually an A-frame, as you can see, with two legs and a plumb bob hanging from

263
00:19:18,761 --> 00:19:21,100
its apex.

264
00:19:21,101 --> 00:19:27,180
There's a mark in the center that tells where, when the object is level.

265
00:19:27,181 --> 00:19:34,120
You level a small area with this and erect two posts, perhaps seven feet apart.

266
00:19:34,121 --> 00:19:36,860
You mount a board on the post.

267
00:19:36,861 --> 00:19:44,160
You could take another post of equal height to the first two and place it at a distance.

268
00:19:44,161 --> 00:19:52,660
Using the board as a siding rod, you could then cut drafts in the bedrock until the pole

269
00:19:52,661 --> 00:19:58,200
is at a level with the first two poles in the small area that you've cut.

270
00:19:58,201 --> 00:20:01,540
Let's call it the leveling area.

271
00:20:01,541 --> 00:20:06,640
This process could then be repeated until the entire base of the pyramid was leveled.

272
00:20:06,641 --> 00:20:10,980
Yet while experts may disagree on the methods used to level the Great Pyramid, they all

273
00:20:10,981 --> 00:20:14,720
agree that the results were remarkable.

274
00:20:14,721 --> 00:20:18,160
They did an astonishing job on leveling the Great Pyramid.

275
00:20:18,161 --> 00:20:26,300
It deviates by less than an inch from the northern corner to the southern corner, which

276
00:20:26,301 --> 00:20:30,360
is a fantastic accomplishment.

277
00:20:30,361 --> 00:20:32,200
The next stage was laying out the base.

278
00:20:32,860 --> 00:20:35,440
This was important because the orientation of the pyramid

279
00:20:35,441 --> 00:20:37,300
was crucial for religious reasons.

280
00:20:37,840 --> 00:20:42,260
You often read about how closely the pyramids are aligned

281
00:20:42,261 --> 00:20:45,500
to the cardinal points, north, south, east, and west,

282
00:20:46,260 --> 00:20:50,500
because just as the pyramid points heavenward towards the sky,

283
00:20:50,501 --> 00:20:53,500
the sky is also anchored in the earth by the pyramids

284
00:20:53,501 --> 00:20:56,860
because the Egyptians associated the cardinal points

285
00:20:56,861 --> 00:21:02,900
with the circuit of the sun, with the sky, and with various constellations.

286
00:21:03,320 --> 00:21:08,200
The generally accepted theory is that the pyramids were oriented using stellar observations.

287
00:21:09,300 --> 00:21:14,360
By bisecting the angle between the points where a particular star rises and sets on the horizon,

288
00:21:14,940 --> 00:21:16,920
a direction of true north can be obtained.

289
00:21:16,921 --> 00:21:21,500
But other experts, including Martin Eisler, believe that a different method was used instead.

290
00:21:21,501 --> 00:21:29,920
It's very hard to point to a distant star and get the accuracy that they were able to achieve.

291
00:21:30,760 --> 00:21:35,660
I think they oriented the pyramids by means of the shadows cast by the sun.

292
00:21:36,640 --> 00:21:41,360
First, the builders would have placed a perpendicular stake called a gnomon in the ground.

293
00:21:42,680 --> 00:21:48,720
When equal and opposite angles of the sun cast shadows over a gnomon,

294
00:21:48,721 --> 00:21:53,720
the bisection of that angle gives you a north-south direction.

295
00:21:55,720 --> 00:21:59,720
Whatever the method, the Great Pyramid was aligned almost perfectly,

296
00:21:59,721 --> 00:22:04,720
its sides having a variance of less than 1 15th of 1 degree from the cardinal points.

297
00:22:04,721 --> 00:22:09,720
Other measurements were equally precise, each side measuring 756 feet,

298
00:22:09,721 --> 00:22:13,720
with a discrepancy of only 4 inches per side.

299
00:22:13,721 --> 00:22:20,760
In order to actually construct this gigantic atavis, the Egyptians faced an equally gigantic

300
00:22:20,761 --> 00:22:23,760
problem in human resources management.

301
00:22:23,761 --> 00:22:28,720
Contrary to the popular image, labor was not performed by hundreds of thousands of slaves,

302
00:22:28,721 --> 00:22:35,400
but by peasant conscripts as part of a corvée, that is, as labor owed the state by the peasants,

303
00:22:35,401 --> 00:22:38,040
probably as part of their taxes.

304
00:22:38,041 --> 00:22:41,340
People are taken from the farms from all over Egypt.

305
00:22:41,341 --> 00:22:43,900
They are brought to a centralized spot, let's say Giza.

306
00:22:43,901 --> 00:22:50,060
They work together in organized gangs directed by experienced masons and architects

307
00:22:50,061 --> 00:22:54,840
to build this great pyramid, which becomes a symbol of national identity.

308
00:22:56,480 --> 00:23:01,260
The workforce lived and died in its own workers' city, right at the site,

309
00:23:01,560 --> 00:23:04,900
which was only discovered and excavated in the early 1990s.

310
00:23:05,460 --> 00:23:11,060
Based on the evidence of our discovery of the size of the Workman camp,

311
00:23:11,061 --> 00:23:17,200
We believe only 20,000 workmen were actually involved in the construction of the pyramid.

312
00:23:17,680 --> 00:23:23,420
We believe that there was permanent workmen, and this could be like 6,000.

313
00:23:24,140 --> 00:23:29,640
Those are actually the workmen who make statues, making technical work,

314
00:23:29,760 --> 00:23:32,460
draftsmen, craftsmen, and things like this.

315
00:23:32,700 --> 00:23:37,380
But the workmen who move the stones could be 15,000, and they work in rotation.

316
00:23:37,780 --> 00:23:39,240
They change them every three months.

317
00:23:39,241 --> 00:23:44,240
These workers were further divided into smaller units, called crews.

318
00:24:09,240 --> 00:24:10,526
tribe, group.

319
00:24:10,527 --> 00:24:18,740
Each group consists of 200 workmen, and they had names like great ones, green ones,

320
00:24:19,340 --> 00:24:24,102
and after that they divide them to small units, 10 to 12 workmen.

321
00:24:24,103 --> 00:24:26,080
Near the workers' quarters,

322
00:24:26,460 --> 00:24:28,728
their graveyard has also been discovered.

323
00:24:28,729 --> 00:24:31,660
One of the overseers' tombs was even made in the form

324
00:24:31,661 --> 00:24:36,984
of a small pyramid, complete with an underground passageway and burial chamber.

325
00:24:36,985 --> 00:24:37,860
The overseers

326
00:24:37,861 --> 00:24:43,260
lived a relatively long life, but the common laborers who died at the site did so at significantly

327
00:24:43,261 --> 00:24:44,260
younger ages.

328
00:24:44,261 --> 00:24:50,500
All the age of the workmen were between 30 to 35 years old.

329
00:24:50,501 --> 00:24:54,240
But the artisans, their ages were between 40 to 45.

330
00:24:54,241 --> 00:24:57,380
And this gives us an idea about the levels of the workers' community.

331
00:24:57,381 --> 00:25:02,020
The artisans were not carrying stones, were not pulling up stones, they were just carving,

332
00:25:02,021 --> 00:25:03,380
and it was a light work.

333
00:25:03,381 --> 00:25:05,160
These people were buried down.

334
00:25:05,620 --> 00:25:08,700
They worked hard work in constructing the pyramids.

335
00:25:09,200 --> 00:25:13,180
The workers were kept supplied by a small army of merchants and tradesmen

336
00:25:13,181 --> 00:25:15,680
who transported their goods to Giza by boat.

337
00:25:16,400 --> 00:25:21,060
Also, in order to feed them, farms have to be established.

338
00:25:21,280 --> 00:25:24,760
New areas of land have to be cultivated in order to do this.

339
00:25:24,840 --> 00:25:28,080
There has to be a means of taking the products, the food,

340
00:25:28,160 --> 00:25:31,340
and getting them to Giza and storing them there and redistributing them.

341
00:25:31,341 --> 00:25:37,040
But immense projects such as the Great Pyramid had far larger effects than simply building

342
00:25:37,041 --> 00:25:39,340
a monument for a dead king.

343
00:25:39,341 --> 00:25:44,840
In some ways you can say that the Great Pyramid building projects built the Egyptian state

344
00:25:44,841 --> 00:25:49,440
because an enormous amount of organization was involved.

345
00:25:49,441 --> 00:25:53,240
It was their space program, it was the Apollo program of the past, where get all of these

346
00:25:53,241 --> 00:25:56,840
people to work together so they can feel great and they can have an accomplishment.

347
00:25:56,841 --> 00:25:59,140
And they did, and it became one country since then.

348
00:26:01,340 --> 00:26:07,500
It would be this workforce that would complete the largest engineering project yet undertaken in human history.

349
00:26:15,940 --> 00:26:23,620
The workers who quarried the majority of the building stones for the pyramids at Giza labored at a site that has only recently been cleared of debris.

350
00:26:23,621 --> 00:26:36,485
Actually, all the stones for building the Great Pyramid came from a location of a quarry next door to the Great Pyramid, about 600 feet from the base of the pyramid.

351
00:26:36,486 --> 00:26:38,980
And we discovered this location.

352
00:26:38,981 --> 00:26:44,340
There's a grid pattern in the ground from which blocks were removed.

353
00:26:44,480 --> 00:26:47,020
There were trenches built in grids.

354
00:26:47,540 --> 00:26:48,980
They dig down the trenches.

355
00:26:49,140 --> 00:26:50,720
They were about two feet wide.

356
00:26:51,240 --> 00:26:54,760
And then they split a layer of stone off.

357
00:26:54,761 --> 00:27:01,080
And they did this layer by layer so that there's a bluff above the pyramid now

358
00:27:01,081 --> 00:27:03,620
that must be 20, 25 feet high.

359
00:27:03,621 --> 00:27:10,420
Workers used simple copper tools, like these preserved in the Cairo Museum, to rough out

360
00:27:10,421 --> 00:27:12,420
the limestone blocks.

361
00:27:12,421 --> 00:27:21,740
Afterwards, using tools much like these, masons smoothed the sides and edges of the blocks.

362
00:27:21,741 --> 00:27:27,920
Then employing wooden sleds and ropes such as this, the blocks were dragged to the pyramid.

363
00:27:27,921 --> 00:27:32,660
Most scholars believe that the workers piled up the blocks in sequential horizontal layers,

364
00:27:32,661 --> 00:27:35,420
with each layer being precisely squared and leveled

365
00:27:35,421 --> 00:27:37,840
until the top of the pyramid was reached.

366
00:27:37,841 --> 00:27:40,200
But again, not all the experts agree.

367
00:27:41,300 --> 00:27:44,640
On the one, the slightest imperfection on the bottom

368
00:27:44,641 --> 00:27:49,040
would assume a cumulative effect as it reached the top.

369
00:27:50,180 --> 00:27:53,820
In addition, when I looked at the Great Pyramid,

370
00:27:53,821 --> 00:27:56,320
I could see that almost none of the courses

371
00:27:56,321 --> 00:27:57,960
were truly level.

372
00:27:57,961 --> 00:28:02,660
And I realized then that this was not the way to go.

373
00:28:02,661 --> 00:28:04,440
There had to be another approach.

374
00:28:05,320 --> 00:28:09,080
And this is what I devoted many years to finding.

375
00:28:10,460 --> 00:28:12,880
Using the collapsed pyramid as a prototype,

376
00:28:13,400 --> 00:28:16,660
Eisler proposes that most pyramids built after the collapsed pyramid

377
00:28:16,661 --> 00:28:18,960
consist of three stages of construction.

378
00:28:20,020 --> 00:28:22,200
First, an inner stepped core,

379
00:28:22,720 --> 00:28:24,980
not unlike the core of the collapsed pyramid was built.

380
00:28:25,540 --> 00:28:27,400
Then, the base was enlarged,

381
00:28:27,401 --> 00:28:29,920
and the large steps were progressively filled in

382
00:28:29,921 --> 00:28:31,740
with a second layer of smaller steps,

383
00:28:31,741 --> 00:28:34,500
creating a shape much closer to a true pyramid.

384
00:28:35,180 --> 00:28:40,180
Finally, the small stepped exterior was encased in a layer of finely finished limestone,

385
00:28:40,680 --> 00:28:42,540
creating a true smooth-sided pyramid.

386
00:28:44,400 --> 00:28:47,580
Because so much of the original stone remains on the Great Pyramid,

387
00:28:48,280 --> 00:28:52,120
it is impossible to tell if this three-stage process was used in its construction.

388
00:28:53,360 --> 00:28:57,940
But as evidence for his theory, Eisler points to a number of smaller pyramids,

389
00:28:57,941 --> 00:29:02,100
called Queen's Pyramids, that stand alongside the large pyramids at Giza,

390
00:29:02,500 --> 00:29:04,360
and which were built at about the same time.

391
00:29:05,740 --> 00:29:10,900
And the remains there show a nucleus of large steps,

392
00:29:11,240 --> 00:29:15,640
which were then filled in with what we call packing stones,

393
00:29:15,760 --> 00:29:19,160
which were smaller stones and brought it to a more pyramidal shape.

394
00:29:19,860 --> 00:29:25,040
And finally, this whole thing was encased with a mantle of smooth limestone

395
00:29:25,041 --> 00:29:26,840
that met at a point in the top.

396
00:29:27,940 --> 00:29:32,440
There are also several theories concerning how the stones were raised to the height of

397
00:29:32,441 --> 00:29:34,700
the upper courses.

398
00:29:34,701 --> 00:29:38,660
Most of them involve the construction of ramps that either spiraled around the pyramid as

399
00:29:38,661 --> 00:29:42,580
it grew or led directly to the current level of construction.

400
00:29:42,581 --> 00:29:48,940
The ramp that the Egyptians used to construct the pyramid was huge, constructed of stone

401
00:29:48,941 --> 00:29:50,440
rubble and mud.

402
00:29:50,441 --> 00:29:55,900
Then when the Egyptians would dismantle this ramp, they had to leave evidence under the

403
00:29:55,901 --> 00:29:57,740
ground of that ramp.

404
00:29:57,741 --> 00:30:00,640
I did excavate on the south side,

405
00:30:00,920 --> 00:30:06,100
and the surprise was discovering a small section of the ramp.

406
00:30:06,760 --> 00:30:09,440
But some experts, including Martin Eisler,

407
00:30:09,900 --> 00:30:11,820
believe that if a large ramp was employed,

408
00:30:12,580 --> 00:30:14,300
its remains would be far more evident.

409
00:30:14,600 --> 00:30:21,560
You can't erase a ramp that may have reached the height of 500 feet.

410
00:30:21,840 --> 00:30:24,960
The remains would be obvious by aerial photography.

411
00:30:24,961 --> 00:30:30,400
Mr. Eisler does think ramps were used, but only on the lower, more easily accessible

412
00:30:30,401 --> 00:30:32,560
levels of a pyramid.

413
00:30:32,561 --> 00:30:37,400
For the higher stages, he believes another method was employed.

414
00:30:37,401 --> 00:30:43,840
I believe they got the stones up the pyramid by putting a slideway of planks along the

415
00:30:43,841 --> 00:30:51,460
side and pulling the stone by a crew of men who are located up the pyramid and snubbing

416
00:30:51,461 --> 00:30:55,420
the rope around a log which is further up the pyramid.

417
00:30:56,040 --> 00:30:59,100
The whole process can be aided considerably

418
00:30:59,101 --> 00:31:04,020
by using levers as the stone is elevated

419
00:31:04,021 --> 00:31:07,080
so that with the aid of levers

420
00:31:07,081 --> 00:31:10,860
and with the aid of crews of men standing uphill,

421
00:31:10,861 --> 00:31:14,500
I believe they could pull the stones into position.

422
00:31:16,360 --> 00:31:18,000
Indeed, at many pyramids,

423
00:31:18,420 --> 00:31:19,660
notches in the building stones

424
00:31:19,661 --> 00:31:22,720
which may have been used for the placement of levers are still visible.

425
00:31:23,540 --> 00:31:27,700
Though controversial, the idea of pulling and levering the stones into place

426
00:31:27,701 --> 00:31:30,620
does address the shortcomings of the various ramp theories.

427
00:31:31,120 --> 00:31:35,200
But as of yet, no one knows for certain exactly how the ancient Egyptians,

428
00:31:35,900 --> 00:31:38,000
a society that didn't even possess the wheel,

429
00:31:38,540 --> 00:31:40,500
accomplished this remarkable feat of engineering.

430
00:31:42,140 --> 00:31:43,960
Once all the blocks were in place,

431
00:31:44,160 --> 00:31:47,200
the Great Pyramid was capped with a triangular piece of granite,

432
00:31:47,201 --> 00:31:51,880
meant to represent the sacred Benmen stone, symbol of the primordial mound,

433
00:31:52,600 --> 00:31:57,840
much like this one which was found at Giza, which may have been coated with a hammered-on layer of gold.

434
00:31:58,640 --> 00:32:02,260
Then, using the same methods that were employed to raise and place the other stones,

435
00:32:02,680 --> 00:32:06,600
the body of the pyramid was covered with a casing of fine white limestone.

436
00:32:07,960 --> 00:32:12,780
Shimmering in the desert sun, the final appearance of the pyramid must have been spectacular,

437
00:32:13,600 --> 00:32:16,140
giving those who worked on it an immense sense of pride.

438
00:32:16,900 --> 00:32:24,820
Its final height was 481 feet, making it the tallest structure in the world until the Cologne Cathedral of Germany topped it in 1880.

439
00:32:24,900 --> 00:32:30,920
The pyramid contains an estimated 2.3 million blocks of stone, each weighing up to 2.5 tons.

440
00:32:32,620 --> 00:32:37,200
Many archaeologists believe that the entire process took almost 23 years.

441
00:32:38,540 --> 00:32:43,780
As impressive as the exterior of the Great Pyramid is, its interior is equally remarkable,

442
00:32:43,781 --> 00:32:48,440
As in the case of all earlier pyramids, there are a series of tunnels and chambers.

443
00:32:49,300 --> 00:32:54,080
The interior arrangements of the pyramid of Khufu are elaborate.

444
00:32:54,620 --> 00:33:01,500
There are three burial chambers, and it is not agreed today whether there are supposed to be three.

445
00:33:02,100 --> 00:33:05,460
It was intended from the beginning, or whether they were revising their plans.

446
00:33:06,120 --> 00:33:11,140
Because of these multiple chambers, and the fact that the pyramid was looted of its contents long ago,

447
00:33:11,141 --> 00:33:14,540
no one knows for certain exactly where Khufu was buried.

448
00:33:15,600 --> 00:33:18,420
But what is believed to be the true burial chamber of the king

449
00:33:18,421 --> 00:33:20,620
is reached by a long passageway,

450
00:33:20,940 --> 00:33:23,280
which then turns into an immense ascending gallery,

451
00:33:23,980 --> 00:33:25,620
the so-called Grand Gallery,

452
00:33:26,380 --> 00:33:31,020
a corbelled passageway nearly 150 feet long and 26 feet high.

453
00:33:31,360 --> 00:33:34,600
Corbelled means that the headers or short ends of the blocks

454
00:33:34,601 --> 00:33:36,900
get successively closer and closer and closer,

455
00:33:36,901 --> 00:33:40,140
so you're looking at a sort of inverse stairway,

456
00:33:40,141 --> 00:33:43,340
which finally comes together way up in the distance on high.

457
00:33:44,280 --> 00:33:47,640
Finally, the grand gallery leads to the heart of the Great Pyramid.

458
00:33:48,380 --> 00:33:53,020
Because this chamber is the only one of the three within the pyramid that contains a sarcophagus,

459
00:33:53,660 --> 00:33:57,040
most scholars believe that this is where King Khufu was actually buried.

460
00:33:58,140 --> 00:34:01,500
It is lined entirely with granite. That's a wonderful thing.

461
00:34:01,980 --> 00:34:08,280
Granite being a stone that was charged with solar associations because it's red for one reason.

462
00:34:08,281 --> 00:34:19,480
30 feet by 15 feet and nearly 18 feet high, the roof of the king's chamber consists of several massive granite slabs, each weighing more than 70 tons.

463
00:34:21,240 --> 00:34:27,900
Above this, there are also a series of false roofs that were designed to reduce the enormous weight pressing down on the chamber from above.

464
00:34:28,380 --> 00:34:31,420
They have worked brilliantly for over 40 centuries.

465
00:34:32,760 --> 00:34:36,880
Khufu had willed the construction of the greatest engineering marvel of the ancient world.

466
00:34:36,881 --> 00:34:41,880
However, the completion of the Great Pyramid didn't end the building on the Giza Plateau.

467
00:34:41,881 --> 00:34:46,880
Indeed, it was only the beginning.

468
00:34:46,881 --> 00:34:52,880
The second large pyramid on the Giza Plateau was built by Khufu's son, King Khafre.

469
00:34:52,881 --> 00:34:59,880
Begun around 2520 BC, it was constructed using the same basic methods as those employed on the Great Pyramid.

470
00:34:59,881 --> 00:35:09,280
Its base is 705 feet on each side, and it rises to a height of 471 feet, only 10 feet

471
00:35:09,281 --> 00:35:11,720
shorter than the Great Pyramid.

472
00:35:11,721 --> 00:35:16,440
But because it is built on ground that is 33 feet higher than the Great Pyramid, it appears

473
00:35:16,441 --> 00:35:19,400
to be even taller.

474
00:35:19,401 --> 00:35:24,540
Due to its smaller base, it also rises at a sharper angle than the Great Pyramid, over

475
00:35:24,541 --> 00:35:28,880
53 degrees to the Great Pyramid's 51 degrees.

476
00:35:28,881 --> 00:35:33,480
And another reason it looks different is that at the very top of it, we have the original

477
00:35:33,481 --> 00:35:36,560
fine casing stones that have been left there.

478
00:35:36,561 --> 00:35:38,900
So it has a very different profile.

479
00:35:38,901 --> 00:35:42,060
The inside of Khafre's pyramid is different as well.

480
00:35:42,061 --> 00:35:47,080
Unlike the Great Pyramid's complex system of tunnels, shafts and chambers, Khafre's

481
00:35:47,081 --> 00:35:51,980
pyramid has a relatively simple internal plan, consisting of two passageways that converge

482
00:35:51,981 --> 00:35:58,380
on a single burial chamber.

483
00:35:58,381 --> 00:36:06,560
The colossal statue behind me represents Mycerinus or Menkaure, who built the third pyramid and

484
00:36:06,561 --> 00:36:09,720
pyramid complex at the Giza Necropolis.

485
00:36:09,721 --> 00:36:18,200
With a base of 335 by 343 feet, Menkaure's pyramid rises to a height of 213 feet, at

486
00:36:18,201 --> 00:36:23,500
an angle of slightly more than 51 degrees, making it considerably smaller than Khafre's

487
00:36:23,501 --> 00:36:24,500
pyramid.

488
00:36:24,501 --> 00:36:28,200
It also has a building mass less than one-tenth that of the Great Pyramid.

489
00:36:28,201 --> 00:36:32,140
But it only looks small when you compare it with those, because the pyramid of Menkaura

490
00:36:32,141 --> 00:36:33,140
is huge.

491
00:36:33,141 --> 00:36:35,220
It's as big as the step pyramid.

492
00:36:35,221 --> 00:36:40,100
The pyramid has the distinction of being clad in its bottom-most courses of granite rather

493
00:36:40,101 --> 00:36:44,220
than limestone, so it would have been more colorful.

494
00:36:44,221 --> 00:36:48,740
Why the pyramids at Giza got progressively smaller remains unknown.

495
00:36:48,741 --> 00:36:53,240
But scholars speculate that the enormous cost of these massive building projects simply

496
00:36:53,241 --> 00:36:57,840
could not be sustained by the kings who followed Khufu.

497
00:36:57,841 --> 00:37:02,840
Of all the monuments connected to the pyramids, perhaps none is as remarkable as the mighty

498
00:37:02,841 --> 00:37:05,740
Sphinx.

499
00:37:05,741 --> 00:37:11,040
A huge stone lion with the head of a man, its design, construction and purpose has been

500
00:37:11,041 --> 00:37:14,180
the source of heated controversy for centuries.

501
00:37:14,181 --> 00:37:19,560
The Sphinx, I think, is compelling because it's this part human, part animal form and

502
00:37:19,561 --> 00:37:23,620
it provides this mysterious air, it's not quite completely all there.

503
00:37:23,621 --> 00:37:27,700
this huge structure on the Giza Plateau, what in the world could it have been about?

504
00:37:28,580 --> 00:37:37,620
We know that from where it is that most likely it was done during the time of the builder of the

505
00:37:37,621 --> 00:37:40,103
second pyramid, King Khafra.

506
00:37:40,104 --> 00:37:45,780
And this is because of the orientation of the Sphinx itself. And

507
00:37:45,781 --> 00:37:51,620
some people even perceive that the face of the Sphinx itself looks like the statue of King Khafra.

508
00:37:53,620 --> 00:38:01,360
240 feet long and 66 feet tall, the Sphinx was the first truly colossal piece of sculpture

509
00:38:01,361 --> 00:38:02,501
in ancient Egypt.

510
00:38:02,502 --> 00:38:07,400
Its builders began by quarrying a U-shaped ditch out of a solid limestone

511
00:38:07,401 --> 00:38:08,854
base of the Giza Plateau.

512
00:38:08,855 --> 00:38:12,460
Then, they sculpted the body from the block of stone that made

513
00:38:12,461 --> 00:38:16,660
up the center of the U, making the bulk of the monument actually part of the plateau

514
00:38:16,661 --> 00:38:17,361
itself.

515
00:38:17,362 --> 00:38:22,400
A recent theory makes an even more direct link between the Sphinx and the natural

516
00:38:22,401 --> 00:38:23,101
landscape.

517
00:38:23,102 --> 00:38:27,920
I really think if you look at the setting of the sphinx, you can see that it is

518
00:38:27,921 --> 00:38:33,983
more part of that environment than most people give it. Why do I say that?

519
00:38:33,984 --> 00:38:35,540
It's because we see

520
00:38:35,541 --> 00:38:42,320
things like that are most identical to it in the western desert due to total natural erosion.

521
00:38:43,440 --> 00:38:50,520
These natural features are called mud lions. This here is a yardang, or a mud lion,

522
00:38:50,521 --> 00:38:52,240
or a natural sphinx.

523
00:38:53,180 --> 00:38:56,680
There are huge numbers of these in the Western Desert in Egypt,

524
00:38:57,400 --> 00:38:59,300
and that's why I'm saying that the ancient Egyptians

525
00:38:59,301 --> 00:39:03,220
may have borrowed that shape from this natural landform,

526
00:39:03,720 --> 00:39:06,920
knowing that it is part of the majesty of the land.

527
00:39:08,060 --> 00:39:11,360
For hundreds of years, there have been rumors of secret tunnels

528
00:39:11,361 --> 00:39:12,980
and chambers within the sphinx.

529
00:39:13,440 --> 00:39:16,400
Not long ago, those ideas were put to the test

530
00:39:16,401 --> 00:39:17,940
with ground-penetrating radar.

531
00:39:17,941 --> 00:39:25,740
And they did survey the whole area, and it proved that there is nothing really underneath the Sphinx.

532
00:39:26,200 --> 00:39:31,420
The Sphinx is a solid rock, or a living rock, and there is nothing really is hidden inside.

533
00:39:31,820 --> 00:39:42,547
A sense of how ancient and revered this monument is can be gained from the fact that its first restoration was undertaken almost 1,100 years after Khafre died, by

534
00:39:42,548 --> 00:39:45,460
Pharaoh Thutmose IV, in and around 1400 B.C.

535
00:39:45,461 --> 00:39:51,480
A 12-foot-tall, 15-ton granite stele erected by Thutmose at the site

536
00:39:51,481 --> 00:39:55,740
relates the story of how, as a young prince, but not the crown prince,

537
00:39:56,240 --> 00:39:58,480
he was on a hunting expedition near the Sphinx.

538
00:39:59,320 --> 00:40:02,860
Pausing for a rest, he fell asleep in the shadow of the statue's head,

539
00:40:03,320 --> 00:40:07,660
which was then the only part of the monument that remained above the encroaching desert sands.

540
00:40:07,661 --> 00:40:10,380
And the Sphinx came to him in his dream and said,

541
00:40:10,520 --> 00:40:14,160
My son Thutmose, I'm dying because of the sand.

542
00:40:14,161 --> 00:40:19,240
If you remove the sand away from my head, I will make you the king of Egypt.

543
00:40:20,060 --> 00:40:22,840
And this prince actually became the king of Egypt.

544
00:40:23,260 --> 00:40:27,660
Then Thutmose, as a matter of fact, is the first archaeologist on earth.

545
00:40:28,140 --> 00:40:30,440
And he's the first one who restored the Sphinx.

546
00:40:31,340 --> 00:40:34,540
The Sphinx was built to guard the pyramids for all eternity.

547
00:40:35,280 --> 00:40:39,500
And yet as imposing as it is, it has failed to ward off the predations of man.

548
00:40:40,300 --> 00:40:43,080
Because of the fabulous riches contained within the pyramids,

549
00:40:43,081 --> 00:40:48,760
all of them, without exception, were broken into and looted by tomb robbers long ago.

550
00:40:50,840 --> 00:40:56,200
Indeed, in many cases, later archaeologists were able to find the interior passageways of a pyramid

551
00:40:56,201 --> 00:41:02,580
simply by following the tunnels dug by thieves, such as this one in Menkares' tomb at Giza.

552
00:41:03,500 --> 00:41:07,160
Some have speculated that this is the reason why the building of large pyramids

553
00:41:07,161 --> 00:41:10,220
declined so rapidly after the golden age of the Fourth Dynasty.

554
00:41:10,640 --> 00:41:12,180
Others take a different view.

555
00:41:13,080 --> 00:41:18,720
I personally believe that they stopped doing the pyramids because the country was unified.

556
00:41:19,560 --> 00:41:29,000
Because if we agree to this premise that Khufu had actually gotten these people to feel great about building this together and becoming one people,

557
00:41:29,700 --> 00:41:34,400
then from there on it became a matter of burial site and that's no big deal.

558
00:41:34,800 --> 00:41:37,240
So they started making little ones.

559
00:41:37,241 --> 00:41:42,740
The attention shifted from the size of the pyramid to the development of the complexes

560
00:41:42,741 --> 00:41:47,840
attached to the pyramid, so that the pyramid temples expanded and became more complicated,

561
00:41:47,841 --> 00:41:50,440
and the valley temples expanded and became more complicated.

562
00:41:50,441 --> 00:41:58,840
After that, the pyramids got increasingly smaller, and it just sort of faded out.

563
00:41:58,841 --> 00:42:04,440
Ironically, most of these later pyramids, such as the Black Pyramid at Daschur, were

564
00:42:04,441 --> 00:42:11,840
made with a core of mud bricks to speed their construction, then faced with a layer of limestone.

565
00:42:11,841 --> 00:42:17,100
Once the limestone was plundered for other construction projects, they quickly eroded,

566
00:42:17,101 --> 00:42:26,400
leaving them in a greater state of disrepair than the earlier stone pyramids.

567
00:42:26,401 --> 00:42:31,540
As long as the pyramids have stood, and as much as they have been studied, new research

568
00:42:31,541 --> 00:42:34,920
promises to unravel even more of their ancient riddles.

569
00:42:34,921 --> 00:42:40,180
New techniques of analysis and investigation are developed and can be applied, and we find

570
00:42:40,181 --> 00:42:46,540
out new things all the time because of that, so that the pyramid field is never exhausted.

571
00:42:46,541 --> 00:42:53,160
Yet, even as new theories are examined and old ones discarded, the pyramids will continue

572
00:42:53,161 --> 00:42:55,620
to maintain their compelling allure.

573
00:42:55,621 --> 00:42:59,780
I remember when I first saw the pyramids, it was in fact as a graduate student I was flying

574
00:42:59,781 --> 00:43:02,920
and to do research, and our plane passed over them,

575
00:43:02,960 --> 00:43:05,400
and I saw them from the air, and I've got to admit, I got teary-eyed.

576
00:43:06,400 --> 00:43:09,060
And I'm sure that we'll know more,

577
00:43:09,140 --> 00:43:12,180
because I always say to people that you never know

578
00:43:12,181 --> 00:43:14,520
what the sand of Egypt might hide of secrets.

579
00:43:15,880 --> 00:43:19,820
Some secrets of the pyramids may remain locked within their stony embrace

580
00:43:19,821 --> 00:43:22,700
until even they succumb to the passage of time.

581
00:43:23,300 --> 00:43:26,600
But even as new knowledge sheds light on their ancient mysteries,

582
00:43:26,601 --> 00:43:30,620
the Egyptian pyramids will remain as eternal monuments,

583
00:43:31,340 --> 00:43:32,860
not only to a bygone era,

584
00:43:33,560 --> 00:43:37,620
but to the boundless ambition and ingenuity of humankind.

